  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several materials and fabrication strategies for stretchable or flexible electronic structures. It begins by highlighting common intrinsically conductive polymers, including polyaniline, PEDOT, and polypyrrole, indicating the chemical basis for soft electronic conductors. The central theme is then how rigid or liquid conductors can be organized into deformable formats.\n\nSeveral schematics show patterning a conductive material on an elastomer mold and then sealing it, creating a soft device in which deposited metal or liquid metal pathways follow a serpentine layout that can bend and stretch without breaking. Other panels compare approaches based on dispersed particles or rod-like fillers embedded in a matrix, suggesting composites that maintain conductivity under deformation.\n\nA second set of drawings emphasizes structural design: metal deposition onto pre-shaped or textured substrates, transfer of 2D micro- and nanostructures onto stretched silicone through selective bonding, and formation of free-standing wavy or buckled patterns after release. These wavy, horseshoe-like, and serpentine geometries are shown as key motifs that convert tensile strain into out-of-plane or in-plane shape changes.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that stretchable electronics can be achieved either by using soft conductive materials or by engineering conductive networks and geometries that accommodate large mechanical strain.",
